# Six defects in ExoPlaSim's aerosol transport. DUST-3 item 1, plus what looking
# for it turned up.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. `aerocore.f90` and `aeromod.f90` are the only code in
# ExoPlaSim that moves an aerosol, and this project is about to put a dust
# emission scheme into them (`aeolian/notes/in-model-dust.md`). Everything
# downstream of that is uncalibratable until these are right, which is why the
# design note puts them first. All six are LATENT rather than harmless: every run
# this project has made sets `L_AERO = 0` in `plasim_namelist`, `aero_main` is
# called only when `nsela == 1 .and. nkits == 0 .and. l_aero > 0`, and so
# `aerocore` has never executed. They bite the moment anything turns it on.
#
# NO NAMELIST SWITCH, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Every other patch in this stack
# defaults to a no-op because both arms of an A/B have to come from one binary
# (`WORKFLOW.md` A3). These are not new terms; they are arithmetic that is wrong,
# and a switch whose "off" position is the wrong answer is the mis-framing
# `CLAUDE.md` names under "do not offer a defect as a decision". The A/B that
# applies here is different and stronger: on every configuration this project
# currently runs, a binary with this patch must produce output BIT-IDENTICAL to
# one without it, because the code it changes is unreachable. That is a test that
# can fail, and it is the one to run before anything else.
#
# BASE. Pristine ExoPlaSim 3.4.2. Neither file is touched by any other patch in
# `exoplasim/patches/`, so this does not interact with the resident stack and in
# particular does not touch `radmod.f90`.
#
#   aerocore.f90  726bdac5a779261f7ad6e38adbc5fafd8d57a4c6ec004010aaf78ce3af770ebc
#   aeromod.f90   c5784a6cee9f188678b0f2fb551e6990961aa406e093048eb40388c95e52aeac
#
# RESIDENT-DESTINED, NOT YET RESIDENT. It is listed in `PENDING_PATCHES` in
# `exoplasim/scripts/rebuild_binaries.py`, and it moves to `RESIDENT_PATCHES` in
# the same commit that applies it and rebuilds every binary. That list means
# "applied to the vendored source right now", so listing it earlier would report
# a problem that is not one.
#
# WHAT IT FIXES, and what each one is worth.
#
#  1. `viscos`: `(4/25)` is integer division and evaluates to 0, so the
#     collision-integral temperature dependence of the Chapman-Enskog viscosity
#     vanished entirely. The check has a right answer rather than a plausible
#     one: with the exponent restored, `mu` for N2 at 288 K comes out 1.77e-5
#     Pa s against the measured 1.76e-5, and without it 1.48e-5. Stokes settling
#     goes as 1/mu, so it was 13 to 21 per cent fast over 200-320 K.
#
#  2. and 3. `mmr2n` and `n2mmr`: `(4/3)` is integer division and evaluates to 1,
#     so the sphere volume was 4/3 too small and the number density exactly 4/3 =
#     33.3% too high. `radmod` builds aerosol optical depth from that number
#     density, so the shortwave optical depth was 33.3% too high with it.
#
#  4. The gravitational settling flux never carried the timestep. `gsettle`
#     returns kg/m2/s; the FFSL fluxes fx, fy and fz carry dt inside their
#     Courant numbers and gz did not, so one model step applied one SECOND of
#     settling. At the 2700 s step this project runs, sedimentation was weaker
#     than intended by that factor. The settling Courant number after the fix is
#     0.004 for a 2 um particle in the bottom layer and 0.36 for a 20 um one, so
#     the corrected term is comfortably stable at this step; that is worth
#     knowing because "it would have gone unstable" is the only defence the
#     omission could have had.
#
#  5. The bottom level was updated TWICE. `do k=2,NL` already covered k=NL, and
#     the "Now for k=nl" block below it then applied fx, fy and fz to that level
#     a second time. The two settling terms cancelled exactly (-gz(nl) from the
#     loop, +gz(nl) from the block), so nothing ever left the atmosphere: dust
#     settling out of the bottom layer was returned to it. That is the whole
#     reason the 99%-per-step sink at the bottom exists, and it is why removing
#     that sink is not safe until this is fixed. The loop now stops at NL-1 and
#     the bottom block takes gz(nl-1) in and gz(nl) out, which is dry deposition
#     by sedimentation.
#
#  6. The two polar cap rows had the fy and fz terms and no settling term at all,
#     at every level, so aerosol fell everywhere except at the caps.
#
#  7. `aero_main` gathered its boundary fields in the MODEL's latitude order and
#     handed them to `aerocore`, whose tracer array is flipped south-to-north by
#     `plasim.f90` on the way in. So `l_source = 2`'s land mask, and `l_source =
#     1`'s solar zenith angle, drove the aerosol source in the WRONG HEMISPHERE.
#     This world's land is not hemispherically symmetric, so the test is direct:
#     one step with `l_source = 2` and a nonzero `fcoeff`, and the bottom-level
#     mixing ratio must correlate with the land mask and not with its mirror.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT. There is no deposition velocity here, no wet
# scavenging and no emission scheme: those are separate patches with their own
# namelist switches. And upstream defect 1 of the design note -- `radmod`'s own
# `apart` never being populated from the namelist -- is NOT fixed here, because
# it lives in `radmod.f90`, which another branch is editing. It stays open.
#
# REPORTED UPSTREAM alongside the two the design note already listed.
#
--- a/aerocore.f90
+++ b/aerocore.f90
@@ -247,7 +247,7 @@
 ! activated if the user set "fill" to be true.
